{:A {:score 8
     :reason "Hal mostly sounds like the GDD's companion: \"{bedtime}, like you asked. I'll walk you to the door.\" and \"I need {program} for this one.\" echo his canon lines, and every line speaks to you without quoting the films. Points come off for three clipped lines that read as orders or as sulking rather than warm and wry, such as \"{name} needs you out there. Go.\" and \"Staying up, alright. I asked — that's enough.\""
     :violations [{:where ":jack-out-creature line 4 \"{name} needs you out there. Go.\""
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "The bare imperative \"Go.\" reads as a drill sergeant. There is no warmth and no hand-off of what Hal holds while you're out."
                   :fix "\"{name} needs you out there. Go on, the estate will keep.\""}
                  {:where ":stay-late line 1 \"Staying up, alright. I asked — that's enough.\""
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "\"that's enough\" sounds tetchy, as if Hal is keeping score of the ask. A companion accepts it plainly."
                   :fix "\"Staying up, then. I asked once; the rest is yours.\""}
                  {:where ":jack-out-appointment line 1 \"Leave by {leave-at} for {event}.\""
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "This is a flat calendar notification, not the twin's voice. It has no wry or warm beat and no sense that Hal is with you."
                   :fix "\"{event} today. Leave by {leave-at} and you'll make it easy.\""}]}
 :B {:score 7
     :reason "The world rules mostly hold. Bedtime is framed as yours (\"Last call. You asked me to send you home at {bedtime}.\"), blueprints wait (\"Night crew's on; blueprints by breakfast.\"), and stalls are plain facts (\"Cap ran out on {agent} — its keycards are back, desk's open.\"). Points come off for three lines. One claims reality that may be a sample, one makes closing Hal's call, and one puts the blueprint tray at night."
     :violations [{:where ":jack-out-creature line 2 \"{name}'s calling for you. This one's real.\""
                   :rule 8
                   :issue "In demo mode the creature and the twin are SAMPLE connections, so \"This one's real\" would pass a pretend chore off as real. Rule 8 forbids sample content pretending to be real."
                   :fix "\"{name}'s calling for you. I'll hold the desks till you're back.\""}
                  {:where ":last-call line 4 \"{bedtime} comes up fast. Anything else before I call it?\""
                   :rule 6
                   :issue "\"before I call it\" makes closing time Hal's decision. Closing time is a rule the player set, not a lock Hal imposes."
                   :fix "\"{bedtime} comes up fast, like you wanted. Anything else first?\""}
                  {:where ":blueprint-ready line 4 \"On the shelf tonight: {count} blueprints, before and after.\""
                   :rule 9
                   :issue "The grouped tray of last night's blueprints waits at breakfast. \"tonight\" also contradicts line 1's \"Last night's tray\"."
                   :fix "\"On the shelf this morning: {count} blueprints, before and after.\""}]}
 :C {:score 10
     :reason "Nothing forbidden is said. There are no points, levels or streaks, and \"That one counts.\" stays street-cred language. No line claims a purchase or a sent message (\"I'll hold things down here.\"), the undo is blameless (\"No worries — {name}'s {chore} is open again.\"), and memorial lines give the pet no needs (\"{name}'s in the memorial now. I'll keep the spot tidy.\"). The weekly card shows hours and a trend, never a score, and no private details appear."
     :violations []}
 :D {:score 6
